Riverpod + Clean Architecture in Flutter (3.0+)
A practical, opinionated field guide with diagrams, code, and test patterns you can keep coming back to.
TL;DR (what you’ll build) A clean feature slice where the UI watches state, application logic orchestrates use-cases, and repositories abstract data sources.
Riverpod is both the DI container (how things are wired) and the reactive state layer (how UI reacts to changes).
We’ll use AsyncNotifier + AsyncValue (modern Riverpod) instead of hand-rolling loading/error strings; it’s clearer and harder to misuse.
Why Riverpod fits Clean Architecture Clean Architecture goals
Separation of concerns: Presentation ↔ Application/Use-cases ↔ Domain ↔ Data.
Inversion of dependencies: upper layers depend on abstractions, not concrete tech.
Testability: everything can be mocked/overridden.
How Riverpod maps
Providers = dependency graph. You expose abstractions (e.g., UserRepository) and inject implementations at the “edge.”
Notifier/AsyncNotifier = application state + orchestration. They call use-cases, transform domain models into UI-ready state.
Ref + overrides = testability. Swap out real repositories with fakes in tests or different build flavors.
Opinionated stance: Prefer AsyncNotifier (or Notifier) over StateNotifier for screen logic; prefer AsyncValue over custom loading/error flags. It’s the idiomatic Riverpod 2.x way and reduces boilerplate.
High-level flow (diagram) --- config: theme: neo-dark --- flowchart LR subgraph P["Presentation_Widgets"] View["ConsumerWidget/HookConsumerWidget"] end subgraph A["Application_State_Orchestration"] VM["AsyncNotifier"] UseCase["UseCase"] end subgraph D["Domain"] Entity["Entities_immutable"] RepoAbstraction[("UserRepository_abstract")] ValueObjects["Value_Objects"] end subgraph I["Data_Implementations"] RepoImpl[("UserRepositoryImpl")] SourceDB[("SQLite/Drift_Firebase")] SourceHTTP[("REST_gRPC")] Cache[("Hive_SharedPrefs")] end View -- watch --> VM VM -- calls --> UseCase UseCase --> RepoAbstraction RepoAbstraction -- implements --> RepoImpl RepoImpl --> SourceDB & SourceHTTP & Cache & Entity VM -- emits --> View Rules of the road
UI never imports Data. It depends only on the VM (Notifier) state and domain/use-case contracts.
Data layer implements repositories and can mix DB, HTTP, and cache.
Riverpod providers tie this together and allow overrides for tests.
Folder structure (feature-first, scalable) lib/ app.dart main.dart core/ error/failure.dart http/http_client.dart features/user/ domain/ entities/user.dart repositories/user_repository.dart usecases/get_user.dart data/ datasources/user_remote_ds.dart repositories/user_repository_impl.dart mappers/user_mapper.dart presentation/ providers/user_providers.dart // Riverpod wiring vm/user_vm.dart // AsyncNotifier-based VM pages/user_page.dart widgets/user_header.dart
Opinionated stance: Organize by feature (not by layer globally). Inside each feature, keep domain/data/presentation subfolders. This scales better in real apps.
Core dependencies you’ll want pubspec.yaml (key ones) dependencies: flutter: sdk: flutter flutter_riverpod: ^2.5.1 freezed_annotation: ^2.4.1 json_annotation: ^4.9.0 dio: ^5.7.0 # or http
drift + sqlite if needed, or firebase_core + cloud_firestore, etc. dev_dependencies: build_runner: ^2.4.13 freezed: ^2.5.7 json_serializable: ^6.9.0 riverpod_generator: ^2.4.0 custom_lint: ^0.6.5 riverpod_lint: ^2.3.9
Opinionated stance:
Use freezed for immutable models.
Use riverpod_generator to reduce boilerplate (@riverpod annotations generate providers).
Add riverpod_lint early; it protects you from common mistakes.
Domain layer (pure Dart, no Flutter) 5.1 Entity
// features/user/domain/entities/user.dart import &lsquo;package:freezed_annotation/freezed_annotation.dart&rsquo;; part &lsquo;user.freezed.dart&rsquo;; part &lsquo;user.g.dart&rsquo;;
@freezed class User with _$User { const factory User({ required int id, required String name, String? email, }) = _User;
factory User.fromJson(Map&lt;String, dynamic&gt; json) =&gt; _$UserFromJson(json); }
5.2 Repository abstraction
// features/user/domain/repositories/user_repository.dart import &lsquo;package:riverpod/riverpod.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../entities/user.dart&rsquo;;
abstract class UserRepository { Future getUserById(int id); }
5.3 Use-case (optional but recommended for complex flows)
// features/user/domain/usecases/get_user.dart import &lsquo;../entities/user.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../repositories/user_repository.dart&rsquo;;
class GetUser { final UserRepository _repo; GetUser(this._repo);
Future call(int id) =&gt; _repo.getUserById(id); }
Why a use-case? Keeps orchestration (validation, combining repos, policies) out of the VM and makes it trivial to test.
Data layer (tech details live here) 6.1 Remote data source (example with Dio)
// features/user/data/datasources/user_remote_ds.dart import &lsquo;package:dio/dio.dart&rsquo;;
class UserRemoteDataSource { final Dio _dio; UserRemoteDataSource(this._dio);
Future&lt;Map&lt;String, dynamic&raquo; fetchUserJson(int id) async { final res = await _dio.get(&rsquo;/users/$id&rsquo;); return res.data as Map&lt;String, dynamic&gt;; } }
6.2 Mapper (decouple API shape from domain)
// features/user/data/mappers/user_mapper.dart import &lsquo;../../domain/entities/user.dart&rsquo;;
class UserMapper { static User fromJson(Map&lt;String, dynamic&gt; json) { return User( id: json[&lsquo;id&rsquo;] as int, name: json[&rsquo;name&rsquo;] as String, email: json[&rsquo;email&rsquo;] as String?, ); } }
6.3 Repository implementation
// features/user/data/repositories/user_repository_impl.dart import &lsquo;../../domain/entities/user.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../../domain/repositories/user_repository.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../datasources/user_remote_ds.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../mappers/user_mapper.dart&rsquo;;
class UserRepositoryImpl implements UserRepository { final UserRemoteDataSource remote; UserRepositoryImpl(this.remote);
@override Future getUserById(int id) async { final json = await remote.fetchUserJson(id); return UserMapper.fromJson(json); } }
Riverpod wiring (providers) + ViewModel Key idea: Expose abstractions to upper layers; bind implementations near composition root or in feature providers so you can override in tests.
// features/user/presentation/providers/user_providers.dart import &lsquo;package:flutter_riverpod/flutter_riverpod.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;package:dio/dio.dart&rsquo;;
import &lsquo;../../domain/repositories/user_repository.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../../domain/usecases/get_user.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../../data/repositories/user_repository_impl.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../../data/datasources/user_remote_ds.dart&rsquo;;
// Low-level client final dioProvider = Provider((ref) { final dio = Dio(BaseOptions(baseUrl: &lsquo;https://api.example.com&rsquo;)); return dio; });
// Data source final userRemoteDataSourceProvider = Provider((ref) =&gt; UserRemoteDataSource(ref.read(dioProvider)));
// Repository binding to abstraction final userRepositoryProvider = Provider((ref) { final ds = ref.read(userRemoteDataSourceProvider); return UserRepositoryImpl(ds); });
// Use-case final getUserProvider = Provider((ref) { return GetUser(ref.read(userRepositoryProvider)); });
7.1 ViewModel using AsyncNotifier
// features/user/presentation/vm/user_vm.dart import &lsquo;package:flutter_riverpod/flutter_riverpod.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../../domain/entities/user.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../providers/user_providers.dart&rsquo;;
// Generated provider when using @riverpod (shown later) // For vanilla: create a manual provider below. class UserVM extends AsyncNotifier { @override Future build() async { // Initial state can be “no user yet”—we’ll keep it pending. // You could also return a cached user here if you have one. throw UnimplementedError(&lsquo;Call load(id) to fetch a user&rsquo;); }
Future load(int id) async { state = const AsyncLoading(); final getUser = ref.read(getUserProvider); state = await AsyncValue.guard(() =&gt; getUser(id)); } }
// Manual provider (no codegen) final userVMProvider = AsyncNotifierProvider&lt;UserVM, User&gt;(() =&gt; UserVM());
Why AsyncNotifier + AsyncValue?
You get loading/error/data handling for free.
AsyncValue.guard wraps exceptions cleanly.
UI stays minimal and declarative.
Alternative (more advanced &amp; less boilerplate): use @riverpod from riverpod_generator to autogenerate providers and keep constructors private. It also enables family parameters elegantly.
With @riverpod (recommended):
// features/user/presentation/vm/user_vm_codegen.dart import &lsquo;package:riverpod_annotation/riverpod_annotation.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../../domain/entities/user.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../providers/user_providers.dart&rsquo;;
part &lsquo;user_vm_codegen.g.dart&rsquo;;
@riverpod class UserVM extends _$UserVM { @override Future build({required int userId}) async { final getUser = ref.read(getUserProvider); return getUser(userId); }
Future refresh() async { final getUser = ref.read(getUserProvider); state = const AsyncLoading(); state = await AsyncValue.guard(() =&gt; getUser(userId: userId)); } }
Then consume with userVMProvider(userId: 42)—no manual load() needed; parameters become part of the provider key.
UI (presentation) // features/user/presentation/pages/user_page.dart import &lsquo;package:flutter/material.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;package:flutter_riverpod/flutter_riverpod.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../vm/user_vm.dart&rsquo; as manual; // if using manual provider // If using codegen: import &lsquo;user_vm_codegen.dart&rsquo;;
class UserPage extends ConsumerWidget { const UserPage({super.key, required this.userId}); final int userId;
@override Widget build(BuildContext context, WidgetRef ref) { // MANUAL provider style: final userAsync = ref.watch(manual.userVMProvider);
// If using codegen + family: // final userAsync = ref.watch(userVMProvider(userId: userId)); return Scaffold( appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('User')), body: userAsync.when( loading: () =&gt; const Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator()), error: (err, st) =&gt; Center(child: Text('Error: $err')), data: (user) =&gt; Padding( padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16), child: Column( crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.start, children: [ Text(user.name, style: Theme.of(context).textTheme.headlineMedium), if (user.email != null) Text(user.email!), const SizedBox(height: 16), ElevatedButton( onPressed: () { // MANUAL style: ref.read(manual.userVMProvider.notifier).load(userId); // Codegen style: // ref.read(userVMProvider(userId: userId).notifier).refresh(); }, child: const Text('Refresh'), ), ], ), ), ), ); } }
App entry with ProviderScope
// lib/main.dart import &lsquo;package:flutter/material.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;package:flutter_riverpod/flutter_riverpod.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;app.dart&rsquo;;
void main() { runApp(const ProviderScope(child: MyApp())); }
A sharper diagram (states &amp; dependencies) [Widget] └─ watch(userVMProvider(userId: 42)) ──────► gets AsyncValue └─ userVM (AsyncNotifier) ├─ ref.read(getUserProvider) ─────► GetUser(use-case) │ └─ ref.read(userRepositoryProvider) ───► UserRepository (abstract) │ └─ UserRepositoryImpl (data) │ └─ UserRemoteDataSource(Dio) │ └─ REST/DB └─ emits AsyncValue back to widget
Error handling &amp; retries (clean patterns) Prefer AsyncValue.guard to wrap exceptions.
Centralize error translation: convert low-level Dio/SQLite errors into domain-level Failure types (optional but clean).
// core/error/failure.dart sealed class Failure { final String message; const Failure(this.message); } class NetworkFailure extends Failure { const NetworkFailure(String m): super(m); } class NotFoundFailure extends Failure { const NotFoundFailure(): super(&lsquo;Not found&rsquo;); }
You can return Either&lt;Failure, T&gt; (with fpdart) from repositories to avoid throwing.
Testing (why Riverpod shines) Override providers in tests to inject fakes.
// test/user_vm_test.dart import &lsquo;package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;package:flutter_riverpod/flutter_riverpod.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;package:myapp/features/user/presentation/vm/user_vm_codegen.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;package:myapp/features/user/presentation/providers/user_providers.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;package:myapp/features/user/domain/entities/user.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;package:myapp/features/user/domain/repositories/user_repository.dart&rsquo;;
class FakeUserRepo implements UserRepository { @override Future getUserById(int id) async =&gt; User(id: id, name: &lsquo;Test&rsquo;, email: &rsquo;t@t.com&rsquo;); }
void main() { test(&lsquo;UserVM loads user&rsquo;, () async { final container = ProviderContainer( overrides: [ userRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(FakeUserRepo()), ], );
final prov = userVMProvider(userId: 1); // codegen/family final sub = container.listen(prov, (_, __) {}); expect(container.read(prov).isLoading, true); final value = await container.read(prov.future); expect(value.name, 'Test'); sub.close(); container.dispose(); }); }
Database choices &amp; wiring SQLite/Drift: local offline-first, SQL safety, migrations.
Firebase/Firestore: real-time sync, simple auth coupling, no SQL.
REST + Cache: typical for existing backends.
Riverpod doesn’t care; only your repository implementation changes.
Tip: If you need streams (e.g., Firestore snapshots), prefer StreamProvider for read-only flows, or wrap inside AsyncNotifier and emit with for await.
Performance &amp; lifecycle tips Use ref.watch in widgets only for what must trigger rebuilds. Use ref.read for one-off calls (e.g., button handlers).
Use families (@riverpod with parameters) for per-id caching/scoping.
Use ref.keepAlive() inside notifiers if you want them to survive off-screen.
Use ref.onDispose to close streams/controllers.
Add riverpod_devtools in debug for time-travel &amp; dependency graph.
Common pitfalls Putting HTTP/DB code in the Notifier. Keep I/O in repositories; VM orchestrates only.
Returning strings as state. Use AsyncValue or a sealed view state (e.g., Freezed union) for complex UIs.
Skipping codegen. You can go manual, but @riverpod reduces mistakes and clarifies intent.
Alternative approaches (and when to pick them) Bloc/Cubit: Mature, verbose, very explicit. Great for teams already standardized on Bloc.
GetIt + Riverpod: Use GetIt purely for DI and Riverpod for state—but Riverpod already is a DI container; I don’t recommend doubling up.
Provider (the older one): Fine for tiny apps; Riverpod is the spiritual successor—safer and more flexible.
My take: For new Flutter 3+ apps aiming at Clean Architecture, Riverpod 2.x + codegen + AsyncNotifier is the current standard, balancing clarity, safety, and ergonomics.
Copy-paste starters 16.1 Minimal main.dart
import &lsquo;package:flutter/material.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;package:flutter_riverpod/flutter_riverpod.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;features/user/presentation/pages/user_page.dart&rsquo;;
void main() =&gt; runApp(const ProviderScope(child: MyApp()));
class MyApp extends StatelessWidget { const MyApp({super.key}); @override Widget build(BuildContext context) { return MaterialApp( theme: ThemeData(colorSchemeSeed: Colors.indigo, useMaterial3: true), home: const UserPage(userId: 1), ); } }
16.2 Replace manual VM with codegen family (recommended)
// features/user/presentation/vm/user_vm_codegen.dart import &lsquo;package:riverpod_annotation/riverpod_annotation.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../../domain/entities/user.dart&rsquo;; import &lsquo;../providers/user_providers.dart&rsquo;;
part &lsquo;user_vm_codegen.g.dart&rsquo;;
@riverpod class UserVM extends _$UserVM { @override Future build({required int userId}) async { final getUser = ref.read(getUserProvider); return getUser(userId); }
Future refresh() async { final getUser = ref.read(getUserProvider); state = const AsyncLoading(); state = await AsyncValue.guard(() =&gt; getUser(userId)); } }
Consume:
// inside UserPage ConsumerWidget final userAsync = ref.watch(userVMProvider(userId: userId));
“How do I connect to the DB?” Implement a datasource that speaks to your DB/HTTP (Drift DAO, Firestore collection, REST client).
Implement a repository that composes those datasources, applies mapping and policies.
Provide that repository via Riverpod (Provider).
The VM/use-case only depends on the repository abstraction. No DB code appears above data layer. Done.
A compact “one-pager” diagram you can screenshot UI (ConsumerWidget) watches: userVMProvider(userId) renders: AsyncValue =&gt; loading/error/data triggers: ref.read(userVMProvider(userId).notifier).refresh()
VM (AsyncNotifier) depends on: GetUser use-case via ref.read(getUserProvider) logic: state = AsyncLoading() -&gt; guard(() =&gt; getUser(userId))
UseCase (GetUser) depends on: UserRepository (abstract) logic: validation/combination/policies, then repo.getUserById(id)
Repository (abstract) contract: Future getUserById(int id)
RepositoryImpl (data) uses: UserRemoteDataSource (Dio/HTTP) + mappers returns: Domain User
DataSource(s) raw IO: REST/DB/Cache
Next steps for your project Add riverpod_generator and convert your screen VMs to @riverpod families.
Adopt AsyncValue consistently for screens that hit I/O.
Introduce freezed entities and (if needed) sealed ViewState when screens get more complex than “one model.”
Write one end-to-end test that overrides the repo provider and asserts the VM state transitions.
Pick your data tech (Drift/Firebase/REST) and implement the repository impl behind the abstraction.
If you paste a specific feature (e.g., “Products” or “Auth”), I’ll scaffold the full slice—entities, use-cases, repository, providers, VM, page, tests—tailored to your stack (SQLite vs Firebase, etc.).
